WEBVTT

00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:07.530
funding for frontline is provided by this station and other public television stations nationwide

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:15.690
and by the corporation for public broadcasting tonight on frontline it's been called the plague

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:30.000
of the 20th century first gay men then hemophiliates then their wives and lovers then children 18 000 people more than half

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:40.430
are dead. Two million more could get it. Tonight, live from Washington, Houston, Denver, and San

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:54.960
Francisco, AIDS, a national inquiry. From the network of public television stations,

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:59.940
presentation of KCTS Seattle, WNET New York.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:10.990
WPBT Miami, WTVS Detroit, and WGBH Boston. This is Frontline with Judy Woodruff.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:20.250
Good evening. This is a special two-hour edition of Frontline. Here at the National Library of

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:25.140
Medicine, we have gathered a panel of distinguished guests. Professor Charles Nesson

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:30.000
of the Harvard Law School will lead a discussion here this evening. We are linked by Sunday,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
satellite to three cities. Houston, where a city is divided by the story we will see tonight.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:42.080
San Francisco, the city that knows most painfully the tragedy of AIDS. And Denver,

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:48.490
a city which has fashioned its own response to an epidemic. In about 10 minutes, we will show you

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:55.850
a documentary film about a person with AIDS. It is an unusual and unique story about one man's

00:01:55.850 --> 00:02:00.000
tragic journey across the United States. It is a film that

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.950
It raises questions about public and personal responsibility, about prejudice and fear.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:11.910
In fact, our biggest fear of AIDS seems to stem from what most of us don't know about

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.800
it: How do you get it?

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.010
Who has it?

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.600
What do we do about it?

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:22.340
First, there are essentially two ways for the AIDS virus to be transmitted: from contaminated

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:30.000
blood products and by certain types of sexual activity in which blood or semen are exchanged.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.260
and needles shared by drug users are the primary ways the disease is spread.

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:40.630
Second, you should know that now 10,000 people have died from AIDS.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:44.320
Another 8,000 people are dying from the disease.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:49.890
Over 150,000 people have ARC, AIDS-related complex.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:53.120
They have some of the symptoms. They are not well.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:56.580
And finally, between 1 and 2 million people

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.000
are believed to have AIDS antibodies in their bloodstream.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:04.740
That would mean in order to have an antibody, they must have had or

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.900
still have the AIDS virus.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.910
But they have no symptoms.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:10.970
They otherwise feel well.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:15.030
Most don't know that they may be carriers.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.850
The important concept to understand is that every year, a

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:22.650
large percentage of the people in each of those groups moves

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:24.430
into the group above.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:28.040
That means in the next year, the death toll will have risen

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.000
from 10,000 to almost

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:39.290
15,000 people. A total of as many as 30,000 will have had AIDS. 250,000 may have ARC, and

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:46.590
over 2 to 3 million will be carrying the antibody and probably the virus. That is the scale

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:53.090
of this epidemic. And who does it affect? 3% are hemophiliacs and blood transfusion

00:03:53.090 --> 00:04:00.000
patients. 8% are heterosexual men and women and children. 17% are

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:07.470
intravenous drug users and 72 percent are gay and bisexual men in fact it is

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:12.640
the gay community devastated by this disease that knows the most about AIDS

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.770
and clearly it is from the gay community that we have to learn how to respond to

00:04:18.770 --> 00:04:25.150
it what human and political resources we need to deal with it Diego Lopez is the

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:30.000
director of clinical services for the gay men's health crisis in New York City

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:35.090
He's a clinical social worker and a psychotherapist in private practice.

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:41.290
Since 1981, he has counseled hundreds of people with the disease, their families, their friends

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:42.970
and lovers.

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:47.670
Professor Charles Nesson will begin our discussion tonight here at the National Institute of

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:49.770
Health.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:53.170
Mr. Lopez, tell us your story.

00:04:53.170 --> 00:05:00.000
Well, I was just recently diagnosed with AIDS less than a month ago, and it's tough

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.500
my life upside down and this I must say that I'm someone who knows a lot about

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:11.820
negotiating with systems about this disease about what it does to people and

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:15.670
I wasn't ready I wasn't prepared sufficiently to deal with the anxiety

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:23.040
the emotional stress the stress to my family and my life partner the

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:28.030
difficulties with telling them myself and they're hearing from me and from my

00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:30.000
own lips that I had capacity

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:36.010
posy sarcoma the difficulty in observing the pain in their faces and in their

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:44.870
reactions was also paramount in the manner in which I kind of dealt with

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:48.850
people it was very important to me it became very important to me that people

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:52.970
hear it from me the people that meant a lot in my life or to hear what's

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:57.090
happening regarding my health from my own lips and not from hearsay or word of

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:00.000
mouth as a result

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:10.860
I've been able to gather a lot of support from my family and my friends, however it still

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:17.370
made my life difficult. I don't know how I'm going to feel from day to day. I can't work

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:24.140
the way I used to. I have a great fear of being dependent and losing control and I've

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:30.000
been a very independent person throughout my life. This is the first time since I've

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:32.140
16 that I can't go to work every day.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:33.140
How old are you now?

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:37.690
I'm 40 years old, I was just 40.

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:44.990
And it just astounds me that my life has changed so drastically in 4 weeks.

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:50.440
And I have to stress that because I've been able to drum up a lot of support and love

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:54.060
and care and I know how to negotiate with hospital systems.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:07:00.000
I've been in a hospital, I really directed the kind of treatment I expected to receive

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.230
and as a result, I received it, but it doesn't...

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:06.230
Can I turn to Dr. Wafse?

00:07:06.230 --> 00:07:12.290
Dr. Wafse, what can medicine do for Mr. Lopez?

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:14.720
I think the first thing medicine can do

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:17.720
is to treat him fairly and without judgment,

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:22.190
to let him know accurately and honestly

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:25.190
what is available and what isn't available,

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:28.310
to be able to say that we can do this,

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:30.000
and to say honestly that we can't,

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.580
can't do that, but allow hope that things have changed so much on a month by month basis

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:41.190
that there may be something in the near future that we can offer that we don't have now.

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:46.170
And most importantly, to recognize that he's an individual and just because so many have

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:52.740
gone through it before, he has to uniquely go through it for himself.

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:58.680
I hear the "we don't have it now" though line very prominently in what you say.

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:00.000
We don't have any answers now.

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:01.940
No.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:09.570
Dr. Krim, what sort of social support does the system have to offer Mr. Lopez?

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:16.870
Well, the system has to offer a lot of things, Mr. Lopez, to start with a system of medical

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:20.440
care that is relevant to his problems and his needs.

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:23.570
I don't believe we have this today in this society.

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:30.000
We have acute care hospitals, we don't have hospitals or institutions who can take care

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.200
patients over the long run of patients who are not necessarily acutely ill for long periods

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:46.000
of time, but nevertheless need medical follow-up, sometimes on very short notice, need supportive

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:51.240
services, legal advice, economic help very often, and so forth.

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:57.550
Mr. Lopez, who has been your support in this? What support have you had?

00:08:57.550 --> 00:09:00.000
My family.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.650
I think because of my openness and honesty, which they kind of taught me to be as a person,

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:11.180
it made it possible for them to understand and accept my sexuality years ago, and it's

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:15.280
also made it possible for them to interact with me regarding my health.

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:18.650
So they've been extremely supportive.

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:24.250
I think my mom especially, I mean, we have a very open relationship and we talk very openly,

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:25.250
and she's furious.

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:26.430
She's angry.

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:27.890
I asked her, "Mom, how do you feel?

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:30.000
You know, is there any sense of guilt or blame?"

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:35.820
that you're feeling or do you think i ought to feel that way and um and her response was no she

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:40.960
goes you've been a good human being you've made a contribution to society i am proud of you i am

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:46.440
angry as a result i think it's unfair you don't deserve to be stricken with this illness this

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:54.000
horrible disease my lover ed has been tremendously supportive um uh we've been together for two years

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:59.410
and um and my concern for him is that could he possibly

00:09:59.410 --> 00:09:59.700
- Okay.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:02.890
have this disease too

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:04.050
and we're living on a

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:04.990
tightrope

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:07.860
right now uh... our relationship

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:08.860
is um...

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:12.100
very supportive of each other but it's also under a great deal of stress right

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:13.030
now

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:16.310
he's living under a tightrope and having to explain to his family that he's

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:18.190
okay

00:10:18.190 --> 00:10:21.570
i've always we've always been embraced by both our families but this time he'll

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:24.790
be going home for easter without me because he needs to do a lot of follow

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:28.110
up work there that's necessary for him to do with his family

00:10:28.110 --> 00:10:30.000
without my presence

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:36.210
Thank you, Mr. Lopez. We'll be coming back to you shortly.

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:42.440
You're about to see a film now about a person who had none of the support that Mr. Lopez

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:47.860
here described. And for that, let me turn you back to Judy Woodruff.

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:53.020
The film you will now see is controversial. That's because it's a portrait of a man with

00:10:53.020 --> 00:11:00.000
AIDS who continued to be promiscuous. In San Francisco and other cities, the organized gay

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.500
is protesting the film because they say that it is unfair to persons with AIDS.

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:10.680
They believe that the general population will be unduly alarmed by this story and conclude

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:15.840
that this man's behavior is the rule rather than the exception.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:21.120
Last July, we started looking at the ways AIDS was beginning to test our society.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:23.610
The implications were startling.

00:11:23.610 --> 00:11:30.000
Schools, health care, fundamental concepts of individual rights and civil liberties,

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:35.710
leaders and in the experts all these seemed at risk we traveled across the

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:40.650
country to find examples of those new problems and began filming several

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:46.910
stories but one of them emerged as something unique it is the story of one

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:53.230
man and how he tested people and institutions wherever he went his is not

00:11:53.230 --> 00:12:00.000
the typical story of a person with AIDS and in our view the choices he made about how to

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:08.050
live his life are rare. But Fabian Calvin Bridges was a real person who tried the limits

00:12:08.050 --> 00:12:15.540
of our system and raised questions for which we must find answers. This is Fabian's story,

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:20.700
and I must warn you, it contains graphic descriptions of sexual behavior.

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:37.680
When we came to Houston, we came looking for a story about AIDS.

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:47.920
We found a city like many others, a health care system overwhelmed,

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:50.520
bewildered doctors and frightened patients.

00:12:54.530 --> 00:12:57.470
When we came to Houston, we didn't know Fabian Bridges.

00:12:57.950 --> 00:12:59.840
He was just one of the faceless victims.

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:05.030
I met Fabian about 1975.

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:10.980
After I got out of school and moved here to Houston in about 1979,

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:15.400
we would keep in contact by phone and letters.

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:17.510
Fabian's friend, Shirley.

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:24.870
Fabian decided that he would like to start a new career and move here and see if Houston would be profitable for him.

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:40.280
Fabian had several jobs after he arrived in Houston, and in 1982, he signed on with a Harris County flood control crew like this one.

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:47.600
At night, he liked to go dancing and listen to music.

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:51.510
He spent time in the nightclubs and gay bars in Houston's Montrose District.

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:58.900
Fabian was 29 years old. He was making close to $19,000 a year, and life seemed promising.

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:01.440
You still waiting for your ampicillin?

00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:02.220
Yeah.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:03.720
Okay, well, they'll get it pretty soon.

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:08.360
Dr. Robert Awe is chief of pulmonary medicine at Jefferson Davis Public Hospital in Houston.

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:10.720
He consults on most AIDS cases there.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:13.930
I first met Fabian Bridges in early April of 85,

00:14:14.510 --> 00:14:17.530
after I'd gotten a call from a doctor in a satellite clinic

00:14:17.530 --> 00:14:22.910
that she thought she had an AIDS patient and wanted to admit him to our hospital for evaluation.

00:14:23.270 --> 00:14:28.020
He came over here and had many AIDS-related infections, did indeed have AIDS,

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:30.000
and was diagnosed here

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:37.980
or four days after his arrival as having aids he was very very sick he had very high fevers he had

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:44.650
persistent diarrhea when i went to texas they told me exactly what condition my son was in

00:14:45.530 --> 00:14:51.700
and how long you know they expect for me to live and everything and for me to try to make a ramus

00:14:51.700 --> 00:14:59.350
for his fume you know because he wasn't going to live long but fabian wasn't ready to die

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:06.230
Like many AIDS patients, his condition improved.

00:15:06.230 --> 00:15:11.310
In July, after three months of Jefferson Davis, Fabian was no longer sick enough to stay.

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:17.040
He'd lost his job, had no money, and there was nowhere for him to live outside the hospital.

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:18.780
Well, Fabian was ready to go home.

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:24.370
And we were trying, we met several times in discharge planning conferences to decide what would be the best.

00:15:24.370 --> 00:15:27.490
And Fabian's desire was to go back to his family in the north.

00:15:31.340 --> 00:15:33.420
and so we paid for a one-way plane ticket

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:37.120
for him to go back there.

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:39.120
- The doctors had done what they could.

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:41.370
The hospital gave Fabian $100

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:47.050
and sent him to his sisters in Indianapolis.

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:51.110
- We just didn't know enough about the disease

00:15:51.110 --> 00:15:54.300
and we knew that when you had it you would die

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:57.970
and that certain aspects of it, it was contagious.

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:06.170
Fabian's two sisters learned what they could about the disease, but with all the

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:10.950
uncertainty surrounding AIDS, the family was afraid.

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:15.080
We weren't sure how you can contact it.

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:20.020
You know, we heard only sexual contact, but now we're hearing other things like saliva

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:25.720
or whatever, and we just didn't know and didn't want to take a chance.

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:30.000
I just couldn't have Calvin in the household with this little bird.

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:37.500
You know, he's a terror around the house, he's in everything, personal belongings.

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:40.390
He even plays in the toilet, you know.

00:16:40.390 --> 00:16:45.680
And with Calvin using the personal things around the house, he would pick them up, you know.

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:48.980
He wouldn't know any difference. He doesn't know what AIDS is.

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:52.470
He doesn't know what homosexuality is. He's innocent.

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:56.580
And I did not want to expose him to that, and I wouldn't.

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:59.670
And I remember him calling one night asking,

00:17:00.250 --> 00:17:07.120
Could he come over and stay? And he was crying. And I remember him saying to her, can I come

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:12.470
over? Do you have a sleeping bag? Can I sleep in your backyard? I think he had a basic need

00:17:12.470 --> 00:17:18.530
of wanting and needing to be around his family. And I think everybody realized that and felt

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:30.000
that. And I think that hurt. It hurt me. It hurt me to say no. With no money, Fabian

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.170
drifted around Indianapolis, sometimes staying in shelters for the indigent. One

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:39.890
day he began showing signs of a gonorrhea infection and went in for

00:17:39.890 --> 00:17:44.680
treatment. That day, about a month after he left Houston, Fabian got in trouble

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:50.270
with the law. Fabian Bridges was arrested for allegedly stealing a bicycle and he's

00:17:50.270 --> 00:17:54.200
brought into the lockup here and he's fingerprinted here and photographed

00:17:54.200 --> 00:17:57.500
right here in his chair. Then he was brought over and placed in the isolation

00:17:57.500 --> 00:18:00.000
cell right here to keep him away from the other

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.430
prisoners so he wouldn't contaminate them or anybody else. When he was arrested

00:18:04.430 --> 00:18:08.970
Fabian told police he had AIDS. Deputy Terry Dale is a medical officer at the

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:13.480
Marion County Jail. I guess my biggest concern was for myself and the other

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:18.280
prisoners and the other deputies that he possibly would come in contact with and

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:26.190
spread the disease. When municipal judge John Downer heard that an AIDS victim

00:18:26.190 --> 00:18:30.000
was being held he left his courtroom to meet with the city prosecutor on the

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:31.780
deputy.

00:18:31.780 --> 00:18:36.020
I think the judge expressed a little concern that possibly other people in the courtroom

00:18:36.020 --> 00:18:44.190
might become frightened if they found out he had AIDS and cause a panic, maybe, or possibly

00:18:44.190 --> 00:18:47.010
even contaminates other people in the courtroom.

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:51.830
The prosecutor readily admitted, yes, we did drop the charges, and he gave his reasons

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:54.470
why, advantages, disadvantages.

00:18:54.470 --> 00:19:00.000
He said it was a minor charge, a misdemeanor theft of a bicycle, we might have reconsidered

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:07.380
it were a felony or a serious violent crime which it wasn't. Kyle Niedepram is

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:12.170
a police reporter for the Indianapolis Star. She covered Fabian's story. They

00:19:12.170 --> 00:19:16.030
were even throwing away some of the pens and pencils that Fabian had used to

00:19:16.030 --> 00:19:19.940
sign some of his documents. So apparently in very short order and I'm

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:24.880
not sure of the time frame, he went to the jail, decision was made quickly, a

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:30.000
little meeting was had and it was decided for best for all to release him

00:19:30.440 --> 00:19:35.960
get him out of town. The charges against Fabian Bridges were dropped and then

00:19:35.960 --> 00:19:40.790
Judge Downer chipped in $20 and the county fund chipped in the rest of

00:19:40.790 --> 00:19:44.950
money. We provided a ticket to Cleveland Ohio for Fabian Bridges to get home to

00:19:44.950 --> 00:19:51.860
his folks because this is where he had for me, where his mother and father lived, so

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:57.000
we made sure that he got the bus ticket and then I transported him to the bus

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:59.160
station and made sure he got on the bus okay.

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:07.870
here's a man who's dying he's just dying you know of aids or whatever we have a dying man

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:15.960
